          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "Your child came in with:\n- two weeks of drinking and weeing a lot\n- 3 kg weight loss\n- two days of vomiting, tummy pain and drowsiness\n\nThe team found that your child has type 1 diabetes. She also had a complication of this called diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). This is when the blood becomes too acidic."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team checked your child's blood sugar, ketones and blood acidity. These were high on arrival. By day 2 they were coming down.\n\nTo treat the DKA, the team:\n- gave fluids through a drip\n- gave insulin through a drip\n- replaced potassium through the drip\n- checked your child every hour\n\nThe intensive care team (PICU) was kept informed. Your child was cared for in the high-dependency area of the ward.\n\nOn day 2, your child moved to insulin injections (a basal-bolus regimen).\n\nA diabetes nurse and a dietician also helped during the stay.\n\nThe team started teaching your family about:\n- carbohydrate counting\n- what to do if blood sugar goes too low (hypo management)\n- what to do when your child is unwell (sick-day rules)\n\nBefore going home, you and your child felt confident giving injections and doing blood sugar checks."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
